## Introduction 

In this report I will be using the Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention Dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository. It contains one row per browsing session on an e-commerce site over a one-year period, with 10 numeric features (page counts and durations, bounce rate, exit rate, page value, special-day proximity) and 7 categorical/ordinal features (month, operating system, browser, region, traffic type, visitor type, weekend flag).

My target is Revenue, a binary True/False flag for whether the session ended in a purchase. I will be comparing three different classifiers on this same target to see which approach handles the problem best: logistic regression, decision tree, kNN (k-nearest neighbor) and then one combined heterogeneous model with them all. This keeps the project focused on a single business question ("will this session convert?") and allows a direct, apples-to-apples performance comparison across algorithms.

I am using these three algorithms because logistic regression gives interpretable coefficients/odds ratios a business stakeholder can act on directly (e.g., "each additional page view increases purchase odds by X%"), decision trees produce visualizable if-then rules that are the most explainable to a non-technical audience, and kNN tests whether a purely instance-based, non-parametric approach can compete once features are scaled. 

Prior work includes Sakar et al. (2018/2019), who used this data to build MLP classifiers, benchmarked against SVM and random forest, optimizing for maximum predictive accuracy. Additionally, in 2026, Yin and Li used this data to compare SVM, decision trees, and logistic regression prediction. My project differs in emphasis from both of these because while I am also using logistic regression and decision trees, with the addition of kNN I'm using simpler, more interpretable models rather than neural nets, prioritizing business legibility and a direct comparison of "classic" algorithms to also get a sense of those algorithms strengths and weaknesses. I will also employ a heterogeneous model to see the efficacy of that. 

My research question is: Can browsing behavior captured during a single e-commerce session (page views, durations, bounce/exit rates, and visitor context) predict whether that session will end in a purchase, and which of three classic classification approaches, logistic regression, decision tree, kNN, or a heterogenous ensemble of all three, best identifies purchase intent given a potential class imbalance?

My evaluation methods will be:  accuracy, precision, recall, F1, and AUC across all three models, compared side by side. Accuracy alone would be misleading if there is a class imbalance, so if that is the case, F1 and AUC will carry more weight in my conclusions.

### Expectations going in 

If there is a class imbalance, it will dominate the results. If there is a class imbalance, I expect F1 and AUC to tell a very different, more honest story than accuracy alone, and I expect this imbalance to affect each algorithm differently: logistic regression and decision trees can often be adjusted for imbalance fairly directly (class weights, threshold tuning), while kNN is more sensitive to imbalance by construction, since majority-class neighbors can simply outvote minority-class ones regardless of true proximity.

PageValues will be the strongest single predictor. Based on general e-commerce intuition (and consistent with what Sakar et al. found), I expect PageValues, a metric tied to pages associated with revenue-generating actions, to carry disproportionate predictive weight across all three models, potentially to the point of overshadowing other features.

The numeric features will be right-skewed and will need transformation. Duration and rate-based columns (BounceRates, ExitRates, the _Duration fields) typically show this pattern in web analytics data, since most sessions are short and low-engagement, with a right-skewed tail of highly engaged sessions. I expect this to matter more for kNN (distance-based, sensitive to scale and outliers) than for the decision tree (distribution-agnostic), with logistic regression somewhere in between.

Some numeric features are likely correlated with each other. I expect the page-count and page-duration pairs (e.g., ProductRelated and ProductRelated_Duration) to be meaningfully correlated, since more pages viewed and more time spent tend to move together. I plan to confirm this in EDA and decide whether that redundancy needs to be addressed or simply accepted depending on the model.

The heterogeneous ensemble will outperform any single model, but probably not dramatically. Combining models with different strengths and blind spots typically smooths out individual weaknesses, but I don't expect a dramatic jump, more a modest, more stable improvement, particularly in recall for the minority class (if imbalanced), which is where I would guess the individual models struggle most.

