```
This dataset has `r ncol(df)` columns with `r nrow(df)` observations. 

Revenue (TRUE/FALSE) is the target variable. 

As noted, there are 10 numeric/continuous features (Administrative, Administrative_Duration, Informational, Informational_Duration, ProductRelated, ProductRelated_Duration, BounceRates, ExitRates, PageValues, SpecialDay), and 8 categorical or ordinal features (Month, OperatingSystems, Browser, Region, TrafficType, VisitorType, Weekend, and the target Revenue).

A few observations are: 

1.) This output shows that there is a class imbalance. Revenue splits into `r sum(df$Revenue == FALSE)` FALSE / `r sum(df$Revenue == TRUE)` TRUE, which works out to ~ `r round(mean(df$Revenue == TRUE) * 100, 1)`% positive. This will be addressed in the data shaping section.

2.) Several numeric features have a significant amount of zeros. The 1st and 3rd quartiles for Administrative, Informational, and their _Duration counterparts are all 0, meaning the majority of sessions never visit those page types at all. This isn't missing data, it's a legitimate behavioral signal (most sessions are product related only), but it will drive strong right-skew and needs to be distinguished from true missingness in the NA/zero check.

3.) VisitorType is dominated by returning visitors. `r sum(df$VisitorType == "Returning_Visitor")` of `r length(df$VisitorType)` sessions are Returning_Visitor, which will matter for how much signal that feature actually carries relative to its imbalance, and is worth keeping in mind if it turns out to be a strong predictor

4.) Structure confirms mostly clean typing, with a few decisions still needed. Numeric fields are read in as int/num appropriately, Weekend and Revenue as logical (will need to be dummy encoded for logistic regression and kNN), and Month and VisitorType are already factors. However, OperatingSystems, Browser, Region, and TrafficType are still stored as raw integers even though they're categorical/nominal codes. These need explicit factor conversion in cleaning so they're not mistakenly treated as continuous by logistic regression or kNN.

This initial structure check doesn't surface any blocking data quality issues, but it does surface two things to explicitly carry into future sections: the zero-inflation in administrative/informational features and the integer-coded categorical variables that need factor conversion before modeling.

#### Target Variable Exploration

Now, I will take a look at the target variable. I will get a sense again of it's classes and plot their distribution to confirm the class imbalance and to make sure there are no other levels. 

```{r Target Variable Exploration}
#Getting class counts
table(df$Revenue)

#Plotting
ggplot(df, aes(x = Revenue, fill = Revenue)) +
  geom_bar() +
  labs(title = "Class Balance of Target Variable: Revenue",
       x = "Revenue",
       y = "Count") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "none")
```
This confirms and visualizes the class imbalance. This means that models trained on the data as-is will tend to be biased toward predicting the majority class (no purchase), which can produce misleadingly high accuracy while performing poorly on the outcome that actually matters for the business question, identifying sessions likely to convert. This affects the three algorithms to different degrees: kNN is particularly sensitive, since majority-class neighbors can simply outvote minority-class ones regardless of true proximity; logistic regression will tend to fit coefficients that favor predicting the majority class unless corrected; and decision trees are somewhat more robust by construction but not immune, since default splitting criteria still favor the majority class.

To address this, I will apply random undersampling of the majority class when training the logistic regression and kNN models, bringing the class ratio closer to balanced. I considered this against oversampling and SMOTE; I chose undersampling primarily for simplicity and because the dataset is large enough that losing majority-class rows still leaves a workable training set. For the decision tree, I will instead address class imbalance using case weights, up-weighting the minority (purchase) class during training so it isn't overwhelmed by the majority class, without discarding any rows. I initially considered a cost matrix, which C5.0 supports natively and which would let me assign an explicit business-interpretable cost ratio to false negatives (missed purchasers) versus false positives. However, C5.0 does not produce valid class probabilities when a cost matrix is applied, which would have prevented me from computing AUC for the tree and broken the consistency of my cross-model evaluation. Case weights avoid this issue, remain compatible with boosting, and keep AUC available across all three models, so I chose weights over a cost matrix for this reason.

