```

Given how small this category is, I considered collapsing it into New_Visitor to simplify the feature, but decided against it: "Other" isn't documented as a variant of "new visitor," and merging the two would impute an identity for those 85 sessions that isn't actually supported by the data. I also considered dropping these rows, but there's no data quality issue here; the category is valid and legitimate, and removing good observations simply because a category is small and inconvenient isn't a defensible reason to shrink the dataset.

Next, I will table the integer categories that are actually categorical data and will be transformed later to look for any placeholder or missing values. 
```{r Interrogating Integer Categorical Data}
table(df$OperatingSystems)
table(df$Browser)
table(df$Region)
table(df$TrafficType)
```
All four variables show sequential, contiguous integer codes with no negative values and no suspicious outlier codes like -1 or 99 sitting apart from the rest. 

One thing worth flagging for later: several of these codes have very low counts (e.g., Browser category 9 has only 1 observation, TrafficType category 12 has only 1, and a handful of others sit in the single digits). This isn't a data quality issue, but it is a sparsity issue worth addressing before encoding: one-hot encoding these as-is would produce several columns with almost no positive cases, adding noise and unstable coefficients, particularly for logistic regression and kNN. I will group rare categories (below 1% of the entire category) into a single "Other" bucket for each of these four variables during feature engineering, applied before the train/test split, and used specifically for the logistic regression and kNN training sets. The decision tree will retain the original, ungrouped categories, since C5.0 splits directly on categorical levels without requiring one-hot encoding, so the sparse-column problem this grouping is meant to solve doesn't apply to that model. I chose to do the grouping on the full dataset rather than separately per split to ensure category groupings stay consistent between training and test data, avoiding a situation where a rare category collapsed in training appears as an unseen level in the test set; since this only affects category definitions and not the target variable, the leakage risk is minimal.

Finally, I will interrogate the numeric paired variables (the _Duration variables), to make sure that a duration is never recorded for a page-type with zero visits (e.g., Administrative == 0 but Administrative_Duration > 0), since that combination would be logically inconsistent

