These skewed numeric variables (Administrative, Administrative_Duration, Informational, Informational_Duration, ProductRelated, ProductRelated_Duration, BounceRates, ExitRates, and PageValues) will need a transformation for both kNN and logistic regression. For both models, I will apply a log1p() transformation rather than plain log, since several of these variables contain true zeros that would otherwise produce undefined values). This compresses the long right tail and brings each variable closer to a usable, roughly symmetric shape without discarding any legitimate high-engagement sessions as outliers.

For kNN specifically, I will additionally z-score standardize the logged variables. This is a separate requirement because these variables currently range from roughly 0–0.2 (BounceRates) up to 0–64,000 (ProductRelated_Duration), and since kNN computes distance directly across features, an unscaled variable like ProductRelated_Duration would dominate every distance calculation and effectively drown out the other features. Standardizing after the log transform ensures each variable contributes comparably to distance, while the log step itself prevents any single extreme value from stretching that standardized distance.

For logistic regression, the log transform alone is sufficient, and I will not additionally z-score these variables for that model, since glm() doesn't rely on distance.

I am not applying outlier removal to these variables prior to transformation. Z-score-based outlier detection assumes a roughly normal distribution, which is precisely what these variables lack in their raw form. Instead, I will detect and manage outliers after transformation. 

