#### Multicollinearity Check

To check for multicollinearity among numeric variables, I will produce a correlation matrix. This is important for the logistic regression model, and done because highly correlated predictors can destabilize coefficient estimates and make them difficult to interpret. It is also relevant for kNN, though for a different reason: kNN has no coefficients to destabilize, but since it computes distance across all features equally, highly correlated variables effectively get double-counted, implicitly overweighting whatever shared signal they represent. The decision tree is the least affected by multicollinearity of the three, since it selects one variable to split on at a time and simply ignores redundant correlated variables.

```{r Multicollinearity Check}
#Building the numeric variable set, excluding SpecialDay since it is now treated as a nominal variable
numeric_vars <- c("Administrative", "Administrative_Duration",
                   "Informational", "Informational_Duration",
                   "ProductRelated", "ProductRelated_Duration",
                   "BounceRates", "ExitRates", "PageValues")

#Corr matrix
cor_matrix <- cor(df[, numeric_vars])
round(cor_matrix, 2)
```
Using a threshold of |r| > 0.7, two pairs of predictors show elevated correlation: ProductRelated and ProductRelated_Duration (r = 0.86), and BounceRates and ExitRates (r = 0.91). Both pairings make intuitive sense, a session with more product-related page views would naturally accumulate more total time on those pages, and bounce rate and exit rate are closely related metrics of session disengagement, so a strong relationship between them isn't surprising.

A handful of other pairs show moderate correlation worth noting but well below the 0.7 threshold: Administrative and Administrative_Duration (r = 0.60), and Informational and Informational_Duration (r = 0.62)- the same page-count-to-duration pattern as ProductRelated, just weaker. BounceRates and ExitRates both also show a consistent negative relationship with PageValues and the page-count/duration variables, which aligns with the earlier boxplot findings that higher bounce/exit rates are associated with lower conversion.

Rather than simply dropping one variable from each pair, I will address this multicollinearity through feature engineering, creating new derived features that capture the shared signal in each pair as a single variable rather than discarding information outright.

For ProductRelated and ProductRelated_Duration, I will engineer an average time per product page feature (ProductRelated_Duration divided by ProductRelated, with zero-page sessions set to zero), which captures engagement intensity rather than raw count or raw time alone. For BounceRates and ExitRates, I will engineer a combined disengagement score, computed as the average of the two rates.

These derived features will replace their source variables for logistic regression and kNN, removing the correlated pairs while preserving the signal they carried. Including both highly correlated variables in either pair would otherwise risk unstable coefficients for logistic regression or overweighted distance contributions for kNN. The decision tree will retain all original variables as-is, since it selects one variable to split on at a time and is not similarly affected by this redundancy.
  
