```
The chi-squared tests confirm what the proportional bar charts suggest visually: most nominal variables show a statistically significant association with Revenue, with Region as the one clear exception.

Statistically significant at the p < 0.05 level:

- Month (x^2 = 384.93, p < 2.2e-16): the strongest result in the set, consistent with the clear seasonal variation seen in the bar chart (November standing out with the highest conversion proportion, February the lowest).
- VisitorType (x^2 = 135.25, p < 2.2e-16): interestingly, New_Visitor shows the highest conversion proportion of the three groups, with Returning_Visitor actually the lowest, which runs counter to an assumption that familiarity with the site would drive more purchases.
- TrafficType (x^2 = 373.15, p < 2.2e-16): significant, though with real spread in conversion rate across categories (TrafficType 16 and 7 notably higher than others).
- SpecialDay (x^2 = 96.077, p < 2.2e-16): significant despite showing no consistent trend in the bar chart, supporting the earlier decision to treat it as nominal rather than ordinal; conversion is actually highest at SpecialDay = 0, not near the special day itself.
- OperatingSystems (x^2 = 75.027, p = 1.416e-13): significant, though the bar chart shows fairly modest differences in conversion proportion across categories.
- Browser (x^2 = 27.715, p = 0.006088): significant, though a weaker relationship than the others, with most categories clustered closely around a similar conversion rate.
- Weekend (x^2 = 10.391, p = 0.001266): significant, with weekend sessions showing a modestly higher conversion rate than weekday sessions.

Not statistically significant:

- Region (x^2 = 9.2528, p = 0.3214): the one nominal variable that fails to show a significant relationship with Revenue. The bar chart confirms this visually: conversion proportion is nearly flat across all nine regions, suggesting geography isn't a meaningful driver of purchase behavior in this dataset.

Given Region shows no significant relationship with the target, it will be excluded, while the remaining nominal variables all show evidence of a meaningful relationship with purchase behavior and will be retained.

### Decision Summary 

For variable management, I will drop Region and convert OperatingSystems, Browser, and TrafficType to factors. SpecialDay will be treated as nominal.

For kNN and logistic regression, the following nominal and logical variables will be dummy encoded: OperatingSystems, Browser, TrafficType, SpecialDay, Month, VisitorType. Because Weekend is already logical, it does not need encoding. All numeric variables will be log1p transformed, followed by z-score standardization for the kNN set specifically.

For feature engineering, nominal variables with rare categories (under 1% of total) will be collapsed into an "Other" bucket. To reduce multicollinearity in kNN and logistic regression, I will derive two engineered features: an average time per product page (ProductRelated_Duration / ProductRelated, with zero-page sessions set to zero) and a combined disengagement score (the mean of BounceRates and ExitRates).

To address class imbalance, I will apply random undersampling of the majority class when training the kNN and logistic regression models, and use case weights with boosting for the decision tree.

## Data Cleaning and Preparation

In this section, I will clean the data, implementing the decisions described in the EDA section. Note that I will only perform overall cleaning and cleaning specific to logistic regression at this stage. This is because completeing the cleaning for kNN requires z-score standardization, and computing that standardization on the full dataset would risk data leakage, since the standardization parameters (mean and standard deviation) would be informed by rows that later become part of the test set. Thus, I will perform the overall clean, then clean for logistic regression (and part of kNN), then split the data, and only then clean fully for kNN using standardization parameters derived from the training set alone. The decision tree does not require any cleaning beyond the overall clean, since the algorithm can handle skewed and non-standardized variables.

### Overall Cleaning 

First, as described in the EDA, I will drop Region, because it shows no significant relationship with the target, and is thus useless to my models. 

```{r Dropping Region}
df$Region <- NULL
```

Next, I will transform OperatingSystems, Browser, and TrafficType to factors.

```{r Transforming Nominal Integers to Factors}
df$OperatingSystems <- as.factor(df$OperatingSystems)
df$Browser <- as.factor(df$Browser)
df$TrafficType <- as.factor(df$TrafficType)
df$SpecialDay <- as.factor(df$SpecialDay)
```

As the target is logical TRUE/FALSE, I also need to transform it to a factor. This is because many of my modeling and evaluation functions in R, including knn(), glm(), C5.0(), and roc(), expect categorical outcomes to be represented as factors rather than logicals. 

```{r}
df$Revenue <- factor(df$Revenue, labels = c("FALSE", "TRUE"))
```

Next, I will apply a 1% frequency threshold, and collapse anything below it into "Other" categories. 

```{r Collapsing Rare Categories, }
vars_to_bucket <- c("OperatingSystems", "Browser", "TrafficType", "SpecialDay")

for (i in vars_to_bucket) {
  counts <- table(df[[i]]) 
  proportions <- counts / sum(counts)
  rare_levels <- names(proportions[proportions < 0.01]) #Finding the rare levels
  
  df[[i]] <- as.character(df[[i]]) #For the column of the name (converting from factor) 
  df[[i]][df[[i]] %in% rare_levels] <- "Other" #Return if there is rare levels, if so make it other
  df[[i]] <- as.factor(df[[i]]) #Convert back into factor
}

#Checking
table(df$OperatingSystems)
table(df$Browser)
table(df$TrafficType)
table(df$SpecialDay)

```

#### Missing and Imputation Exercise 
As noted in the EDA, there is no missing data or placeholders to impute to remove. 

### Algorithm-Specific Cleaning 

In this section, I will copy the data into three different datasets, one for each algorithm, and shape and clean the data for each algorithm accordingly. 

```{r Copying Data for each algorithm}
df_logreg <- df
df_knn <- df
df_tree <- df
```

### Cleaning for Logistic Regression

#### Feature Engineering for Multicollinearity

As noted, I will derive new features by combining each correlated pair, then drop both original variables from each pair in the kNN and logistic regression sets. This produces a single column that captures the combined signal of the two, rather than retaining both correlated variables separately.

```{r Multicollinearity Feature Engineering}
#ProductRelated_AvgTime: average time spent per product-related page
df_knn$ProductRelated_AvgTime <- ifelse(df_knn$ProductRelated == 0, 0, #Creates a new column, sets the value to zero already if ProductRelated is zero
                                          df_knn$ProductRelated_Duration / df_knn$ProductRelated) #if not, divides duration by number of product pages viewed, so it is an everage per page

df_logreg$ProductRelated_AvgTime <- ifelse(df_logreg$ProductRelated == 0, 0,
                                             df_logreg$ProductRelated_Duration / df_logreg$ProductRelated)

#Disengagement_Score: average of BounceRates and ExitRates
df_knn$Disengagement_Score <- (df_knn$BounceRates + df_knn$ExitRates) / 2 #Row-wise average column
df_logreg$Disengagement_Score <- (df_logreg$BounceRates + df_logreg$ExitRates) / 2

#Drop the original source variables
df_knn <- df_knn %>% select(-ProductRelated, -ProductRelated_Duration, -BounceRates, -ExitRates)
df_logreg <- df_logreg %>% select(-ProductRelated, -ProductRelated_Duration, -BounceRates, -ExitRates)
```

#### Transformation of Numeric Variables

Now, I will log1p transform the numeric variables: Administrative, Administrative_Duration, Informational, Informational_Duration, ProductRelated_AvgTime, Disengagement_Score, and PageValues. I do this because these variables are heavily right-skewed, and a log transformation compresses the long tail of extreme values, bringing each variable closer to a shape that better satisfies the assumptions of logistic regression and improves the reliability of distance calculations in kNN. I use log1p() specifically (log(x + 1)) rather than a plain log transform, since several of these variables contain true zero values that would otherwise produce undefined results. As noted, I will only do this for the kNN and logistic regression sets, since the decision tree can handle skewed variables natively and does not require this transformation.

```{r Log1p Transform, echo= FALSE }
#Transform for kNN
df_knn$Administrative_log <- log1p(df_knn$Administrative)
df_knn$Administrative_Duration_log <- log1p(df_knn$Administrative_Duration)
df_knn$Informational_log <- log1p(df_knn$Informational)
df_knn$Informational_Duration_log <- log1p(df_knn$Informational_Duration)
df_knn$ProductRelated_AvgTime_log <- log1p(df_knn$ProductRelated_AvgTime)
df_knn$Disengagement_Score_log <- log1p(df_knn$Disengagement_Score)
df_knn$PageValues_log <- log1p(df_knn$PageValues)

#Transform for Logistic Regression
df_logreg$Administrative_log <- log1p(df_logreg$Administrative)
df_logreg$Administrative_Duration_log <- log1p(df_logreg$Administrative_Duration)
df_logreg$Informational_log <- log1p(df_logreg$Informational)
df_logreg$Informational_Duration_log <- log1p(df_logreg$Informational_Duration)
df_logreg$ProductRelated_AvgTime_log <- log1p(df_logreg$ProductRelated_AvgTime)
df_logreg$Disengagement_Score_log <- log1p(df_logreg$Disengagement_Score)
df_logreg$PageValues_log <- log1p(df_logreg$PageValues)

#Removing untransformed vars
df_knn$Administrative <- NULL
df_knn$Administrative_Duration <- NULL
df_knn$Informational <- NULL
df_knn$Informational_Duration <- NULL
df_knn$ProductRelated_AvgTime <- NULL
df_knn$Disengagement_Score <- NULL
df_knn$PageValues <- NULL
df_logreg$Administrative <- NULL
df_logreg$Administrative_Duration <- NULL
df_logreg$Informational <- NULL
df_logreg$Informational_Duration <- NULL
df_logreg$ProductRelated_AvgTime <- NULL
df_logreg$Disengagement_Score <- NULL
df_logreg$PageValues <- NULL
```

#### Encoding

Now for as mentioned in the EDA, we will dummy encode Month, VisitorType, OperatingSystems, Browser, TrafficType, SpecialDay for the logistic regression and kNN sets. This converts each nominal variable into a set of binary columns, one per category level, so that these algorithms can use the information numerically rather than as unordered category labels.

```{r Encoding}
vars_to_encode <- c("Month", "VisitorType", "OperatingSystems", "Browser", "TrafficType", "SpecialDay")

#kNN 
for (v in vars_to_encode) {
  var_levels <- levels(df_knn[[v]])
  var_levels <- var_levels[-1] #Dropping the first level
  
  for (lvl in var_levels) {
    col_name <- paste0(v, "_", lvl) #joins the variable and the level into a new column 
    df_knn[[col_name]] <- ifelse(df_knn[[v]] == lvl, 1, 0) #If the current variable's value is the level, get a 1
  }
  
  df_knn[[v]] <- NULL #Get rid of initial column
}

#Logistic Regression 
for (v in vars_to_encode) {
  var_levels <- levels(df_logreg[[v]])
  var_levels <- var_levels[-1]
  
  for (lvl in var_levels) {
    col_name <- paste0(v, "_", lvl)
    df_logreg[[col_name]] <- ifelse(df_logreg[[v]] == lvl, 1, 0)
  }
  
  df_logreg[[v]] <- NULL
}
```

### Cleaning for Decision Tree

Minimal additional cleaning is required for the decision tree beyond the overall clean already applied. C5.0 splits directly on categorical variables in their native factor form, so it does not require one-hot encoding, and it makes no distributional assumptions, so the skewed numeric variables do not need a log transform. It is also not sensitive to feature scale, so z-score standardization is unnecessary, and since it evaluates one variable at a time when selecting splits, it is not meaningfully affected by the multicollinearity addressed for logistic regression and kNN, so the original ProductRelated, ProductRelated_Duration, BounceRates, and ExitRates variables are retained rather than replaced with engineered features.

The rare-category grouping applied to OperatingSystems, Browser, TrafficType, and SpecialDay was carried over to df_tree as well, even though C5.0 is less prone to the sparse-dummy-column instability that motivated this step for logistic regression and kNN. This keeps category definitions consistent across all three model-specific datasets and avoids retaining extremely small categories (some with only a handful of observations) that could still produce unstable, overfit splits in the tree. df_tree therefore reflects the state of the data after Overall Cleaning and rare-category grouping, with Region dropped and categorical variables converted to factors, but no log transformation, feature engineering, encoding, or standardization applied. Class imbalance for the decision tree is addressed separately in the Data Splitting section using case weights, applied after the train/test split.

The only additional cleaning needed for the Decision Tree data is to make all logicals factors, as C5.0 cannot digest logicals. 

```{r Decision Tree Cleaning}
#Identifying any logical columns in df_tree
logical_cols <- names(df_tree)[sapply(df_tree, is.logical)]
logical_cols

df_tree$Weekend <- as.factor(df_tree$Weekend)
```


### Data Splitting

Now, I am splitting my data. I am splitting the data into training and test sets for each of the three model-specific datasets using the same row indices across all three. Because the indices are identical, each test set contains the exact same underlying sessions in the same order, just represented with each model's own cleaned features. This makes the three models' predictions directly comparable, and allows the ensemble to be evaluated on any one of the three test sets (since their Revenue values are identical). I am using stratified splitting, which splits each class (TRUE and FALSE Revenue) separately before combining them, ensuring that both the training and test sets preserve the same 84.5% / 15.5% class ratio found in the full dataset, rather than risking an uneven split by chance given how imbalanced the target variable is.

```{r Data Splitting}
set.seed(100)

#Getting row indices for each class
true_indices <- which(df$Revenue == "TRUE")
false_indices <- which(df$Revenue == "FALSE")

#Shuffling each set of indices
true_indices <- sample(true_indices)
false_indices <- sample(false_indices)

#Calculating how many rows go into training (80%) for each class
n_true_train <- round(0.8 * length(true_indices))
n_false_train <- round(0.8 * length(false_indices))

#Splitting each class into train/test
train_true <- true_indices[1:n_true_train]
test_true <- true_indices[(n_true_train + 1):length(true_indices)]

train_false <- false_indices[1:n_false_train]
test_false <- false_indices[(n_false_train + 1):length(false_indices)]

#Combining
train_index <- c(train_true, train_false)
test_index <- c(test_true, test_false)

#Applying to dataframes
train_knn <- df_knn[train_index, ]
test_knn  <- df_knn[test_index, ]

train_logreg <- df_logreg[train_index, ]
test_logreg  <- df_logreg[test_index, ]

train_tree <- df_tree[train_index, ]
test_tree  <- df_tree[test_index, ]
```

### Cleaning for kNN: Z-score Standardization

I will now complete the final cleaning step for the kNN set: z-score standardization. I will use train_knn's mean and standard deviation, and apply them to both the train and test kNN sets. This is because computing standardization parameters from the full dataset (or from the test set) would leak information about the test set's distribution into the training process. Using only the training set's mean and standard deviation, and applying those same fixed values to the test set keeps the test set truly held out while still ensuring both sets are on a comparable scale for kNN's distance calculations.

```{r Z-Score Standardization}
#Identifying the numeric columns 
numeric_cols <- c("Administrative_log", "Administrative_Duration_log",
                   "Informational_log", "Informational_Duration_log",
                   "ProductRelated_AvgTime_log", "Disengagement_Score_log",
                   "PageValues_log")

#Computing mean and sd 
train_means <- sapply(train_knn[, numeric_cols], mean)
train_sds <- sapply(train_knn[, numeric_cols], sd)

#Standardizing 
for (col in numeric_cols) {
  train_knn[[col]] <- (train_knn[[col]] - train_means[col]) / train_sds[col]
}

#Apply to test_knn
for (col in numeric_cols) {
  test_knn[[col]] <- (test_knn[[col]] - train_means[col]) / train_sds[col]
}
```

## Post-transform Exploration

Now that the kNN set has been standardized, I will use it to run three exploratory checks: outlier detection, cluster analysis, and PCA. Outlier detection is run here because standardizing does not change a row's relative position within its distribution, so the results apply equally to the logistic regression set.

Clustering and PCA are also run on the kNN set's standardized features, since both require scaled input. Depending on what PCA reveals about the redundancy in my numeric and encoded features, I may use the resulting principal components as replacement features specifically for the kNN model. kNN benefits from this in a way the other two models do not, because distance calculations become less meaningful as dimensionality increases, since points tend to appear roughly equidistant from one another in high-dimensional sparse space. Reducing this to a smaller number of components that capture most of the variance directly addresses that weakness. 

I will not apply PCA to the logistic regression set, since principal components are not individually interpretable as business variables, which conflicts with logistic regression's role in this project of producing coefficients a stakeholder can act on directly. PCA does not apply to the decision tree at all, since it does not benefit from dimensionality reduction.

#### Outlier Check

Now, I will perform a z-score outlier check. I will do this by detecting observations that are more than 3 standard deviations from the mean (either above or below the mean), which is a standard cutoff for outliers. This check requires log-transformed, standardized numeric data to be meaningful, since a raw-scale z-score check would be distorted by the skew. I will run this detection on the kNN set, which has already been log-transformed and standardized, and apply the same results or any decisions to the logistic regression set, since it shares the same underlying log-transformed values and standardizing does not change a row's position relative to the rest of its distribution. I will not apply this check to the decision tree set, since it retains raw, untransformed numeric variables, and C5.0's split-based structure is inherently less sensitive to extreme values.

```{r Outlier Detection}
#Making empty matrix with T/F whether or not its outlier
outlier_flags_train <- matrix(FALSE, nrow = nrow(train_knn), ncol = length(numeric_cols)) #Starts with FALSE 
outlier_flags_test <- matrix(FALSE, nrow = nrow(test_knn), ncol = length(numeric_cols))

colnames(outlier_flags_train) <- numeric_cols #Setting colnames to the numeric variables 
colnames(outlier_flags_test) <- numeric_cols

#Loops through and flags outliers
for (col in numeric_cols) {
  outlier_flags_train[, col] <- abs(train_knn[[col]]) > 3 #Mark TRUE if the absolute value is more than 3
  outlier_flags_test[, col] <- abs(test_knn[[col]]) > 3
}

#Total number of outlier values found per column
colSums(outlier_flags_train)
colSums(outlier_flags_test)

#Number of rows with at least one outlier value
rows_with_outliers_train <- rowSums(outlier_flags_train) > 0
rows_with_outliers_test <- rowSums(outlier_flags_test) > 0

sum(rows_with_outliers_train)
sum(rows_with_outliers_test)
```
Running the outlier check on the standardized kNN training and test sets flags `r sum(rows_with_outliers_train)` of `r nrow(train_knn)` training rows (~10.2%) and `r sum(rows_with_outliers_test)` of `r nrow(test_knn)` test rows (~11.0%) with at least one value beyond 3 standard deviations. Administrative_log and Administrative_Duration_log show zero flagged values, while the two engineered features, ProductRelated_AvgTime_log and Disengagement_Score_log, show the most.

Consistent with the decision made earlier in this notebook, I am retaining these flagged observations rather than removing them. At roughly 10% of the data, these are not rare, isolated anomalies but a normal feature of this dataset's skewed behavior, and the earlier data quality checks confirmed no evidence of corrupted or impossible values. Removing them would risk disproportionately discarding legitimate high-engagement sessions, which are precisely the kind of session most associated with Revenue = TRUE given PageValues' established relationship with conversion. This would work directly against the minority class I am already taking separate steps (undersampling) to preserve. This check therefore serves as a diagnostic confirming the scale of extreme values present.

#### Clustering Exploration 

I will now explore clustering with k-means clustering. This is an unsupervised method that groups observations into subgroups based on similarity, without using the target variable, allowing me to check whether the data contains natural segments of browsing behavior. I will only do this on my numeric variables, because k-means relies on Euclidean distance, and including the one-hot encoded categorical dummy variables alongside the numeric variables would distort that distance calculation, since binary dummy columns behave differently in distance terms than continuous standardized variables and would disproportionately influence cluster assignment. 

I am performing this clustering exploration on the kNN set specifically, since it is the only one of my three datasets that is both log-transformed and standardized. Since cluster membership is derived using Euclidean distance, I'm also only adding it as an engineered feature to the kNN set, where that distance-based logic is directly relevant to the model.

First, I will begin by using the elbow method to determine my k, or amount of clusters. This computes k-means clustering for a range of possible k values and plots the total within-cluster sum of squares for each; the point where this curve bends and flattens indicates the k value beyond which adding more clusters yields diminishing returns.

```{r Elbow Method}
set.seed(200)

cluster_input <- train_knn[, numeric_cols]

fviz_nbclust(cluster_input, kmeans, method = "wss")
```
This chart shows k=4 as where the line flattens substantially. Thus, I will now conduct k means clustering with k=4. 

```{r K means clustering, echo=FALSE}
#k-means clustering with k = 4
set.seed(123)
final <- kmeans(cluster_input, centers = 4, nstart = 25)

# Print the results
print(final)
```
```{r Visualizing the k menas clusters}
fviz_cluster(final, data = cluster_input)
```
Visualizing the four clusters along the first two principal components (which together explain ~65.6% of the variance in the numeric features) shows moderate separation. Clusters 2 (green) and 4 (purple) sit distinctly on the left side of the plot, with cluster 4 forming the most visually distinct, compact group. Clusters 1 (red) and 3 (teal) show substantial overlap in the middle of the plot, though cluster 3 extends further to the right along Dim1, suggesting it captures sessions with higher values along the underlying pattern Dim1 represents. The point labels visible in the plot are row numbers.

Now that the clusters have been formed, I will examine what each cluster actually represents by calculating the average value of each numeric variable within each cluster. This will let me interpret each cluster in terms of actual behavior rather than treating cluster membership as an unlabeled group. I will then cross-tabulate cluster membership against Revenue to check whether any cluster is disproportionately associated with converting sessions. If a cluster shows a meaningfully higher or lower purchase rate than the overall ~15.5% baseline, that would suggest cluster membership carries predictive signal and is worth considering as an engineered feature. If purchase rates are roughly similar across all four clusters, that would indicate the clusters reflect browsing behavior patterns that are not strongly tied to conversion, and I will not use cluster membership as a feature.

```{r K means average values}
#Average value of each numeric variable per cluster
train_knn %>%
  mutate(Cluster = final$cluster) %>%
  group_by(Cluster) %>%
  summarise(across(all_of(numeric_cols), mean))

#Tabling with Revenue
table(final$cluster, train_knn$Revenue)
```
Cross-tabulating cluster membership against Revenue reveals meaningful differences in conversion rate across clusters. Against an overall baseline conversion rate of ~15.5%, Cluster 3 converts at the highest rate (~24.3%), followed by Cluster 1 (~21.2%), both abive baseline. Cluster 2 converts below baseline (~9.3%), and Cluster 4 stands out sharply, with a conversion rate of just ~0.5% (3 of 652 sessions), suggesting this cluster captures a distinct group of sessions that almost never result in a purchase.

This result, combined with the cluster means, indicates that cluster membership does carry meaningful signal relating to purchase behavior. Given this, I will add cluster membership as an engineered categorical feature for the kNN model.

```{r Adding the clusters assignation as a predictive var}
#Creating a new column officially storing the cluster assignations for the train set
train_knn$Cluster <- as.factor(final$cluster)

#Getting the centroids 
centroids <- final$centers

#Assigning each test row to its closest centroid
assign_cluster <- function(row, centroids) { #New function, taking the row and the centroids
  distances <- apply(centroids, 1, function(centroid) {
    sqrt(sum((row - centroid)^2)) #Taking the euclidean distance
  })
  return(which.min(distances)) #finding the shortest distance
}

test_clusters <- apply(test_knn[, numeric_cols], 1, assign_cluster, centroids = centroids) #apply my function to the whole set 
test_knn$Cluster <- as.factor(test_clusters) #add this as a new column 

```

```{r Checking work, hide=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
table(train_knn$Cluster)
table(test_knn$Cluster)
```
Now the clusters are assigned into to columns in the test and train set. Because the clusters are nominal, I need to dummy encode them: 

```{r}
#Cluster 2
train_knn$Cluster_2 <- ifelse(train_knn$Cluster == "2", 1, 0)
test_knn$Cluster_2 <- ifelse(test_knn$Cluster == "2", 1, 0)

#Cluster 3
train_knn$Cluster_3 <- ifelse(train_knn$Cluster == "3", 1, 0)
test_knn$Cluster_3 <- ifelse(test_knn$Cluster == "3", 1, 0)

#Cluster 4
train_knn$Cluster_4 <- ifelse(train_knn$Cluster == "4", 1, 0)
test_knn$Cluster_4 <- ifelse(test_knn$Cluster == "4", 1, 0)

#Dropping the original Cluster column 
train_knn$Cluster <- NULL
test_knn$Cluster <- NULL
```

Now, the cluster assignation is added as a nominal variable in the kNN set. 

#### Dimensionality Reduction (PCA)

Now, I will perform dimensionality reduction to further optimize my data. I will do this on the kNN set, since it is the only one of my three model-specific datasets that has already been standardized, and because kNN is likely to benefit from a reduced feature space given the sparsity introduced by one-hot encoding. Logistic regression could technically benefit from PCA as well, since it can further reduce noise, but I am not applying it there: logistic regression does not suffer from dimensionality the way distance-based kNN does, and I have already addressed the main multicollinearity concern directly through feature engineering. I will do this on the numeric columns, because because PCA relies on variance calculations that are only meaningful for continuous variables.

I will do this via PCA, which is an unsupervised technique that transforms the original correlated features into a smaller set of new, uncorrelated components, ordered by how much variance in the data each one explains. 

```{r PCA}

pca_result <- prcomp(train_knn[, numeric_cols], center = TRUE, scale. = TRUE)

#Proportion of variance explained
summary(pca_result)
```
Looking at the proportion of variance explained per component, PC1, PC2, and PC3 each carry a substantial share (44.5%, 21.1%, and 16.0% respectively), while PC4 drops to 12.2% and the remaining components (PC5–PC7)  each explain less than 4% individually. Given this, I will retain the first 4 principal components, capturing the large majority of the variance in the numeric feature set while reducing dimensionality from 7 original variables down to 4 uncorrelated components. 

I will create a kNN set with the PCA reduced variables replacing the numeric variables, and retain one without. Thus, I can test which model performs better, and which to add to my overall ensemble. 

```{r}
#Keep only the first 4 
train_pca <- as.data.frame(pca_result$x[, 1:4])
colnames(train_pca) <- paste0("PC", 1:4) #renaming columns 

test_pca <- as.data.frame(as.matrix(test_knn[, numeric_cols]) %*% pca_result$rotation[, 1:4])
colnames(test_pca) <- paste0("PC", 1:4)

#Making PCA-reduced copy
train_knn_pca <- train_knn
test_knn_pca <- test_knn

train_knn_pca <- cbind(train_knn_pca, train_pca) #adding the pca columns 
