## Modeling

### kNN

The first model that I will build is a kNN, or k-nearest neighbors model. This is an instance-based, non-parametric classification algorithm that predicts a session's outcome by identifying the k most similar sessions in the training data (based on distance across all features) and taking a majority vote among their outcomes. Unlike logistic regression or the decision tree, kNN makes no assumptions about the underlying relationship between the features and the target. Its value in this project is testing whether a purely similarity-based approach can compete with the other two once features are properly scaled and encoded.

#### Tuning

I first need to find the best k, or number of nearest neighbors, for both the PCA and raw feature sets. I will do this by tuning for the k value that maximizes cross-validated performance, using 10-fold cross-validation on the balanced training data to test a range of odd k values (to avoid tie votes) and selecting whichever k yields the best average performance across folds. I will tune k separately for the raw and PCA-reduced training sets, since each represents a different feature space. I am choosing 10 folds because it is a standard, widely-used choice that balances reliability and computational cost.

I will start my k's as 1-36, and will expand the range if I need to. 

I am evaluating with AUC because, as noted in my Introduction, accuracy alone would be misleading given the class imbalance in this dataset. AUC also keeps this evaluation consistent with the metric I will use to compare across all three (and later the ensemble) models, allowing an apples-to-apples comparison later in the Evaluation section.

#####Tuning for Raw Set 
```{r Tuning for Raw Set}
set.seed(123)
k_values <- seq(1, 37, by = 2)
n_folds <- 10

#Fold assignments
