#### Feature Set Comparison (Raw vs. PCA)

Now, I will use the test set to make a comparison as to which set is better and should be added to the ensemble. I will test on F1 and AUC, because, as discussed in my Introduction, accuracy alone would be misleading given the class imbalance present in this dataset, and these two metrics each capture a different, more informative view of performance under that imbalance. AUC is threshold-independent, and measures how well the model ranks true purchasers above non-purchasers across all possible classification thresholds. F1, by contrast, evaluates performance at a specific decision threshold (0.5 here) and directly balances precision and recall for the minority "TRUE" (purchase) class, the outcome that matters most for the business question this project is centered on. Using both together guards against a model that ranks well in the abstract (high AUC) but performs poorly at the threshold actually being used to classify sessions, giving a more complete picture of each feature set's real-world usefulness.

```{r F1 Function}
#Creating a function to compute F1 score
f1_score <- function(actual, predicted) {
  tp <- sum(predicted == "TRUE" & actual == "TRUE") #True positive
  fp <- sum(predicted == "TRUE" & actual == "FALSE") #False positive
  fn <- sum(predicted == "FALSE" & actual == "TRUE") #False negative 
  precision <- ifelse((tp + fp) == 0, 0, tp / (tp + fp)) #Precision
  recall <- ifelse((tp + fn) == 0, 0, tp / (tp + fn)) #Recall
  f1 <- ifelse((precision + recall) == 0, 0, 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall))
  return(f1) #Return 0 instead of NA if both precision and recall are 0 (avoids divide-by-zero)
}
```

