#### Bagging

Now, I will apply bagging (bootstrap aggregating) to the kNN model to see whether it improves performance over the single kNN fit evaluated above. Bagging works by drawing many bootstrap resamples (random samples with replacement) from the training data, fitting a separate kNN model to each resample, and then averaging the predicted probabilities across all of these models to produce a final prediction. 

I will fit kNN at k = `r best_k_pca` (the value selected during tuning) across 50 bootstrap resamples of train_knn_pca_balanced, average the resulting predicted probabilities on test_knn_pca, and then recompute AUC and F1 on these averaged probabilities using the same 0.5 threshold applied throughout this project. I anticipate bagging may offer a small boost to kNN's stability and performance, particularly given how sensitive plain kNN is to the specific composition of its training set.

```{r Bagging}

set.seed(700)
B <- 50 

n_train <- nrow(train_knn_pca_balanced)
n_test <- nrow(test_knn_pca)

#Matrix to store predicted probabilities 
bag_probs <- matrix(NA, nrow = n_test, ncol = B) #rows = test obs, cols = bootstrap iteration

#Separating predictors and target
test_x_pca_bag <- test_knn_pca[, !names(test_knn_pca) %in% "Revenue"]

for (b in 1:B) {
  boot_index <- sample(1:n_train, size = n_train, replace = TRUE) #sample and replace
  boot_sample <- train_knn_pca_balanced[boot_index, ] #builds a new sample 
  
  #Splitting into predictors and targets for every bootstrap taregt
  boot_train_x <- boot_sample[, !names(boot_sample) %in% "Revenue"]
  boot_train_y <- boot_sample$Revenue
  
  #Fitting a kNN model on this bootstrap sample
  pred_b <- knn(train = boot_train_x, test = test_x_pca_bag, cl = boot_train_y, k = best_k_pca, prob = TRUE)
  
  #Predicted probabilities
  prob_b <- attr(pred_b, "prob")
  prob_b_yes <- ifelse(pred_b == "TRUE", prob_b, 1 - prob_b) #Making sure its the prob of true
  
  #Storing this iteration's predictions
  bag_probs[, b] <- prob_b_yes
}

#Averaging predicted probabilities across all B bootstrap models
bag_prob_avg <- rowMeans(bag_probs)

#AUC for the bagged model
roc_bag <- roc(response = test_knn_pca$Revenue, predictor = bag_prob_avg,
                levels = c("FALSE", "TRUE"), direction = "<", quiet = TRUE)
auc_bag_test <- auc(roc_bag)

#F1 for the bagged model
pred_class_bag <- ifelse(bag_prob_avg > 0.5, "TRUE", "FALSE")
f1_bag_test <- f1_score(test_knn_pca$Revenue, pred_class_bag)

#Comparing single-fit vs bagged performance
bagging_comparison <- data.frame(
  Model = c("Single kNN (PCA)", "Bagged kNN (PCA)"),
  AUC = c(auc_pca_test, auc_bag_test),
  F1 = c(f1_pca_test, f1_bag_test)
)

bagging_comparison
```
While the bagged version is only marginally different from the single version, it is a stabler model, and thus will be retained. This means that while a single kNN model's predictions could shift meaningfully depending on which specific sessions happened to land in the training set, the bagged model's averaged probabilities are less sensitive to that particular composition, which should make it a more reliable  estimate of purchase intent when applied to new data.

#### Evaluation

Having selected the bagged kNN model on the PCA-reduced feature set (k = `r best_k_pca`, B = 50 bootstrap resamples) as the final kNN configuration, its performance on the held-out test set is summarized below: AUC = `r round(auc_bag_test, 4)` and F1 = `r round(f1_bag_test, 4)`. Both metrics indicate strong performance.

This performance is somewhat stronger than my original expectation from the Introduction, where I anticipated kNN would be particularly vulnerable to the class imbalance in this dataset given its instance-based, majority-vote structure. The combination of undersampling during training, dimensionality reduction and bagging appears to have meaningfully mitigated this vulnerability, positioning kNN as competitive heading into the cross-model comparison with logistic regression and the decision tree in the final Evaluation section.

### Logistic Regression

The second model I will build is a logistic regression model. This is a classification algorithm that models the target outcome (Revenue = TRUE) as a linear combination of the predictor variables, producing interpretable coefficients that describe each feature's relationship with purchase likelihood. Unlike kNN, logistic regression makes explicit distributional and linearity assumptions about how features relate to the log-odds of the outcome, but in exchange it offers something kNN cannot: coefficients that can be converted into odds ratios and communicated to a business stakeholder in plain terms, e.g., "each additional page view increases the odds of purchase by X%." This interpretability is precisely why I selected it for this project, as discussed in my Introduction, and it will serve as the "explainable" counterpart to kNN's purely similarity-based approach.

Because a single logistic regression fit's coefficients can shift, I will build a homogeneous bagged ensemble rather than relying on one fit. I will generate 50 bootstrap partitions (matching B = 50 from kNN) from train_logreg_balanced, fit a logistic regression model to each, and retain each model's out-of-bag observations as that model's evaluation set to tune the cutoff. For each candidate cutoff, I will compute F1 across all 50 models, average the scores, and select whichever cutoff maximizes this average. Unlike kNN's k, which was tuned via 10-fold CV, cutoff is tuned within the bagging procedure itself, since OOB data provides a natural evaluation set.

I will combine the 50 models by weighting each one's predictions according to its own OOB F1 score, so stronger-performing models contribute more than weaker ones. The finalized ensemble will be evaluated once on the untouched test_logreg set for final AUC and F1.

#### Bagging and Tuning

Here, I am bootstrap partitioning and tuning with the boostrap OOB data. 

```{r Bagging and Tuning}

n.partitions <- 50
sample.size <- nrow(train_logreg_balanced)

#Creating a list to hold partitions
partitions <- list()
out.of.bag <- list()

#Generating bootstrap partitions
set.seed(760)
for (i in 1:n.partitions) {
  sampled.indices <- sample(1:nrow(train_logreg_balanced),
                             size = sample.size,
                             replace = TRUE)
  partitions[[i]] <- train_logreg_balanced[sampled.indices, ] #adding the sample to the partitions list
  out.of.bag[[i]] <- train_logreg_balanced[-unique(sampled.indices), ] #adding to the oob list
}

#Building an empty list to record
lr_models <- list()

for (i in 1:length(partitions)) {
  train.data <- partitions[[i]] #Storing the train data
  model <- glm(Revenue ~ ., data = train.data, family = binomial) #Training
  lr_models[[i]] <- model
}

cutoff_candidates <- seq(0.05, 0.70, by = 0.05)
oob_f1_by_cutoff <- numeric(length(cutoff_candidates))

for (c in seq_along(cutoff_candidates)) {
  cutoff <- cutoff_candidates[c]
  
  model_f1s <- sapply(1:length(lr_models), function(i) {
    model <- lr_models[[i]]
    oob.data <- out.of.bag[[i]]
    probs <- predict(model, newdata = oob.data, type = "response")
    preds <- ifelse(probs > cutoff, "TRUE", "FALSE") #Predicting on the cutoff
    f1_score(oob.data$Revenue, preds)
  })
  
  oob_f1_by_cutoff[c] <- mean(model_f1s)
}

cv_results_lr <- data.frame(cutoff = cutoff_candidates, mean_f1 = oob_f1_by_cutoff)
cv_results_lr

best_cutoff_lr <- cv_results_lr$cutoff[which.max(cv_results_lr$mean_f1)]
best_cutoff_lr
```
The best cutoff is .35, which will be locked in as the cutoff variable: best_cutoff_lr. 

#### Homogeneous Ensemble

Now, knowing that the optimal cutoff is 0.35, I will apply it to a homogeneous ensemble. I will rank the 50 bootstrap models against one another by evaluating each model's F1 score on its own out-of-bag data at this tuned cutoff, then normalize these scores into weights so that models with stronger individual OOB performance contribute more heavily to the final ensemble. I will then build the ensemble as a function that combines all 50 models' predicted probabilities into a single weighted average, using these OOB-derived weights.

```{r}
#Calculating model weights
model_weights_f1 <- sapply(1:length(lr_models), function(i) {
  model <- lr_models[[i]] #Pulls out model 
  oob.data <- out.of.bag[[i]] #Pulls the model's oob data
  probs <- predict(model, newdata = oob.data, type = "response")
  preds <- ifelse(probs > best_cutoff_lr, "TRUE", "FALSE") #predict
  f1_score(oob.data$Revenue, preds)
})
weights <- model_weights_f1 / sum(model_weights_f1) # adding weights
data.frame(model = 1:length(lr_models), oob_f1 = model_weights_f1, weight = weights) #display table

#Building the ensemble function
lr.ensemble <- function(models, oob.sets, new.data, cutoff = best_cutoff_lr) { #setting parameters
  
  #Computing F1 for each model using its out-of-bag data
  f1s <- sapply(1:length(models), function(i) {
    model <- models[[i]]
    oob.data <- oob.sets[[i]]
    probs <- predict(model, newdata = oob.data, type = "response")
    preds <- ifelse(probs > cutoff, "TRUE", "FALSE")
    f1_score(oob.data$Revenue, preds)
  })
  
  weights <- f1s / sum(f1s) #Normalizing weights to add to 1
  
  predictions <- sapply(models, function(model) { #Predict with each model on new data
    predict(model, newdata = new.data, type = "response")
  })
  
  if (is.vector(predictions)) { #Making sure predictions are in matrix form
    predictions <- matrix(predictions, nrow = 1)
  }
  
  weighted.probs <- predictions %*% weights #Applying weights
  as.vector(weighted.probs)
}
```

### Evaluation 

Now, I will evaluate my ensemble. 

```{r Logreg Evaluation}
lr_ensemble_probs <- lr.ensemble(lr_models, out.of.bag, test_logreg, cutoff = best_cutoff_lr)
lr_ensemble_preds <- ifelse(lr_ensemble_probs > best_cutoff_lr, "TRUE", "FALSE")

#AUC
roc_lr <- roc(response = test_logreg$Revenue, predictor = lr_ensemble_probs,
               levels = c("FALSE", "TRUE"), direction = "<", quiet = TRUE)
auc_lr_test <- auc(roc_lr)

#F1
f1_lr_test <- f1_score(test_logreg$Revenue, lr_ensemble_preds)

auc_lr_test
f1_lr_test
```

This model achieved an AUC of `r round(auc_lr_test, 4)` and an F1 score of `r round(f1_lr_test, 4)`. The AUC is strong and comparable to kNN's bagged model (AUC = `r round(auc_bag_test, 4)`), indicating that logistic regression, despite its far simpler linear structure, ranks converting sessions above non-converting ones nearly as well as the more instance-based kNN approach. 

The F1 score (`r round(f1_lr_test, 4)`), however, is somewhat lower than kNN's bagged F1 (`r round(f1_bag_test, 4)`), suggesting that at the classification threshold ultimately selected, logistic regression's precision/recall balance on the minority purchasing class is less favorable than kNN's. This gap between AUC and weaker F1 is a useful reminder of why both metrics were tracked throughout this project, a model can rank outcomes well overall while still underperforming at the threshold used to make classification decisions. Since both kNN and logistic regression were trained on undersampled data, this comparison does not directly test how differently the two algorithms handle class imbalance on their own; that distinction will be more meaningfully evaluated once the decision tree, which instead used case weights on the full dataset, is complete.

### Decision Tree

The third model I will build is a decision tree, using the C5.0 algorithm. This rule-based, non-parametric algorithm recursively splits the data into subgroups based on the features that most effectively separate the target classes, producing interpretable if-then rules. Unlike kNN and logistic regression, the tree requires no transformation, standardization, or encoding, since C5.0 splits directly on categorical variables and makes no distributional assumptions, which is why df_tree retains the original variables.

As discussed in Data Cleaning, I will address class imbalance here using case weights rather than undersampling, up-weighting the minority (TRUE) class during training without discarding rows. I considered a cost matrix, since C5.0 supports this natively, but it does not produce valid class probabilities when applied, which would break AUC computation and disrupt consistency across models. 

Because C5.0's trials parameter builds a sequence of trees, each trained to correct the errors of the prior trees, and combines their predictions via a weighted vote, boosting itself constitutes a homogeneous ensemble of decision trees, satisfying the same underlying requirement as bagging while using a combination mechanism native to this specific algorithm. I will therefore prioritize tuning and applying boosting over building a separate manual bagging loop, since it is both more idiomatic to C5.0 and more efficient to implement. I will fit a single case-weighted baseline model, then tune trials to build the final decision tree ensemble for this section.

#### Case Weight Setup 

To address class imbalance, I will assign each observation a case weight based on the inverse frequency of its class, giving the minority "TRUE" (purchase) class a proportionally higher weight than the majority "FALSE" class. These weights will be passed directly into the C5.0 model during training, so that misclassifying a purchasing session is treated as more costly than misclassifying a non-purchasing session.

```{r Case Weights}

class_counts <- table(train_tree$Revenue)
weight_true <- sum(class_counts) / (2 * class_counts["TRUE"])
weight_false <- sum(class_counts) / (2 * class_counts["FALSE"])

case_weights <- ifelse(train_tree$Revenue == "TRUE", weight_true, weight_false)
```

#### Baseline Model 

Before applying boosting, I will first fit a single case-weighted decision tree with no boosting iterations evaluated once on the test set at a standard 0.5 threshold. This baseline model serves as the reference point against which the boosted ensemble will later be compared, allowing me to assess whether boosting meaningfully improves on a single decision tree for this dataset.

```{r Baseline DT}
tree_baseline <- C5.0(Revenue ~ ., data = train_tree, weights = case_weights)

#Predicting probabilities on the test set
tree_baseline_probs <- predict(tree_baseline, newdata = test_tree, type = "prob")[, "TRUE"]

#Converting probabilities to class predictions at a 0.5 threshold
tree_baseline_preds <- ifelse(tree_baseline_probs > 0.5, "TRUE", "FALSE")

#AUC
roc_tree_baseline <- roc(response = test_tree$Revenue, predictor = tree_baseline_probs,
                          levels = c("FALSE", "TRUE"), direction = "<", quiet = TRUE)
auc_tree_baseline <- auc(roc_tree_baseline)

#F1
f1_tree_baseline <- f1_score(test_tree$Revenue, tree_baseline_preds)

auc_tree_baseline
f1_tree_baseline
```

The single case-weighted decision tree achieved an AUC of `r round(auc_tree_baseline, 4)` and an F1 of `r round(f1_tree_baseline, 4)`, evaluated on the test set at a standard 0.5 threshold. This is a notably strong baseline, already outperforming both the bagged kNN ensemble (AUC = `r round(auc_bag_test, 4)`, F1 = `r round(f1_bag_test, 4)`) and the bagged logistic regression ensemble (AUC = `r round(auc_lr_test, 4)`, F1 = `r round(f1_lr_test, 4)`) on both metrics, despite being a single, unensembled model trained on the full, imbalanced dataset rather than an undersampled or bootstrap-aggregated one.

This result is particularly interesting because, as noted, decison trees can often be adjusted for imbalance fairly directly, in contrast to kNN's greater sensitivity to imbalance by construction. The decision tree's strong single-model performance here, achieved purely through case weighting rather than undersampling, offers early support for that expectation. Even before any ensembling is applied, case weighting on the full training set appears to be at least as effective as undersampling was for the other two algorithms, and possibly more so given the tree's higher baseline F1. Whether boosting improves on this already-strong result further remains to be seen in the following subsection.

#### Tuning Trials 

I will tune the number of boosting iterations (trials) using 10-fold cross-validation on train_tree, consistent with my approach for kNN. I begin testing at trials = 5, 10, 15, 20, since this range is wide enough to reveal whether performance is still improving or has started to decline, while keeping the initial search computationally reasonable.

```{r Tuning Trials}
#Tuning trials via 10-fold CV 
set.seed(999)
n_folds <- 10
trials_candidates <- c(5, 10, 15, 20)

folds_tree <- sample(rep(1:n_folds, length.out = nrow(train_tree)))

cv_results_tree <- data.frame(trials = trials_candidates, mean_f1 = NA)

for (t in trials_candidates) {
  fold_f1s <- numeric(n_folds)
  
  for (fold in 1:n_folds) {
    cv_train_tree <- train_tree[folds_tree != fold, ]
    cv_val_tree   <- train_tree[folds_tree == fold, ]
    cv_train_weights <- case_weights[folds_tree != fold]
    
    model <- C5.0(Revenue ~ ., data = cv_train_tree, weights = cv_train_weights, trials = t)
    
    probs <- predict(model, newdata = cv_val_tree, type = "prob")[, "TRUE"]
    preds <- ifelse(probs > 0.5, "TRUE", "FALSE")
    
    fold_f1s[fold] <- f1_score(cv_val_tree$Revenue, preds)
  }
  
  cv_results_tree$mean_f1[cv_results_tree$trials == t] <- mean(fold_f1s)
}

cv_results_tree
best_trials <- cv_results_tree$trials[which.max(cv_results_tree$mean_f1)]
best_trials
```

To ensure the true optimum was not missed at the upper boundary of the initial search, and since F1 continued to improve through trials = 20, I will extend the range of candidate trials values further (c(20, 30, 40, 50)) and re-run the same 10-fold cross-validation procedure.

```{r}
