auc_het_final
f1_het_final
```

The heterogeneous ensemble achieved a final AUC of `r round(auc_het_final, 4)` and F1 of `r round(f1_het_final, 4)` on the test set, evaluated at a tuned cutoff of `r round(best_het_cutoff, 2)`. This represents the highest AUC of any model built in this project, exceeding even the boosted decision tree's AUC of `r round(auc_tree_boosted, 4)`, while its F1 is nearly identical to the tree's (`r round(f1_tree_boosted, 4)`), a difference of only `r round(abs(f1_het_final - f1_tree_boosted), 4)`. This result closely matches my Introduction expectation that the ensemble would outperform any single model, but not dramatically, producing a  more stable improvement rather than a dramatic jump in predictive power.

### Strengths and Weaknesses of Each Model 

kNN performed respectably (AUC =`r round(auc_bag_test, 4)`, F1 = `r round(f1_bag_test, 4)`) once features were log-transformed, standardized, and reduced via PCA, but this performance came at the cost of the heaviest preprocessing burden of the three algorithms, requiring careful handling to be competitive at all. As expected from the Introduction, kNN offers no interpretability, its predictions come from proximity to past sessions rather than any explainable rule, making it the least useful of the three models for generating actionable business insight, despite performing on par with the other algorithms on both metrics.

Logistic regression offers the clearest theoretical interpretability of the three, its fitted coefficients can, in principle, be converted directly into odds ratios that a stakeholder could act on. This report did not extract or interpret those specific coefficients, focusing instead on predictive performance, but this remains a structural advantage of the algorithm that could be pursued in future work. Despite this potential for interpretability, logistic regression produced the weakest F1 score of the three individual models (`r round(f1_lr_test, 4)`) despite a strong AUC (`r round(auc_lr_test, 4)`), suggesting its linear structure ranks sessions by purchase likelihood well overall, but is comparatively less precise at the specific threshold used for classification.

The decision tree, boosted through 30 sequential iterations, was the strongest individual model on both metrics (AUC = `r round(auc_tree_boosted, 4)`, F1 = `r round(f1_tree_boosted, 4)`). Like logistic regression, its rule-based, if-then structure offers a theoretical interpretability advantage over kNN, though this report did not extract the specific split rules generated. Its use of case weights rather than undersampling allowed it to train on the full dataset without discarding any rows, and boosting provided a substantially larger performance gain over its single-tree baseline than bagging did for either kNN or logistic regression, since boosting directly and iteratively corrects prior errors rather than simply averaging over independently resampled fits.

### Best Model and Conclusion

The heterogeneous ensemble achieved the highest AUC of any model built in this project (`r round(auc_het_final, 4)`), narrowly exceeding even the boosted decision tree's AUC (`r round(auc_tree_boosted, 4)`), while its F1 score (`r round(f1_het_final, 4)`) is nearly identical to the tree's alone (`r round(f1_tree_boosted, 4)`), a difference of only `r round(abs(f1_het_final - f1_tree_boosted), 4)`. This result closely matches my Introduction expectation that combining models with different strengths would yield a modest improvement rather than a dramatic jump in performance. Notably, the boosted decision tree alone remains highly competitive with the full ensemble, suggesting that for this dataset, a single well-tuned, boosted tree captures nearly all of the achievable predictive signal on its own.

Given its combination of the strongest overall performance and meaningful (if unexamined in this report) rule-based interpretability, the boosted decision tree stands out as the most practically useful model on its own, while the heterogeneous ensemble offers a small additional edge in ranking ability (AUC) at the cost of losing the tree's relative simplicity. For a business stakeholder prioritizing both accuracy and explainability, the boosted decision tree is likely the more actionable choice; for a use case where even a small gain in predictive accuracy meaningfully matters, such as at very large scale, the ensemble's marginal AUC advantage may justify its added complexity.

Returning to the original research question, browsing behavior captured during a single e-commerce session, particularly PageValues, exit and bounce rates, and product-page engagement, does meaningfully predict purchase intent, and a boosted decision tree or heterogeneous ensemble of classic algorithms identifies this intent well. Consistent with expectations set out at the start of this project, class imbalance shaped every stage of this analysis, from the choice of evaluation metrics to the imbalance-handling strategy used for each algorithm, and PageValues emerged, as anticipated, as a dominant predictor. The comparatively modest gains from ensembling further suggest that for this dataset, the underlying signal already present in the browsing behavior data, rather than the specific choice of algorithm, is the primary driver of predictive performance.

